PRACTICE — lesson 14: free a busy port
--------------------------------------
Your new app must listen on TCP port 5000, but it fails with
"Address already in use". Three servers are running in this
container, on ports 3000, 5000 and 5432. The ones on 3000 and 5432
are needed and must keep running.

1. Find the process that listens on port 5000 and write only its
   PID (just the number) to /work/pid.txt.
2. Stop that process so port 5000 is free. It saves its data when
   it gets the polite SIGTERM, so it must not be killed harder
   than that.

Check: sh /practice/check.sh     Answer: /practice/solution.md
[exit 0]

# check PRZED rozwiązaniem (oczekiwane FAIL)
$ sh /practice/check.sh
FAIL  /work/pid.txt does not exist yet
FAIL  port 5000 is still in use
PASS  the server on port 3000 is still running
PASS  the server on port 5432 is still running

RESULT: FAIL (2 check(s) failed)
[exit 1]

# solution.md
$ lsof -t -i :5000 > /work/pid.txt

[exit 0]

# check po CZĘŚCI rozwiązania (oczekiwane FAIL)
$ sh /practice/check.sh
PASS  pid.txt has the PID of the process on port 5000
FAIL  port 5000 is still in use
PASS  the server on port 3000 is still running
PASS  the server on port 5432 is still running

RESULT: FAIL (1 check(s) failed)
[exit 1]

# solution.md
$ kill $(cat /work/pid.txt); sleep 1

[exit 0]

# check PO solution.md (oczekiwane PASS)
$ sh /practice/check.sh
